WEBVTT

00:00:00.930 --> 00:00:05.850
You're watching this video, thanks to
computer chips, that started their

00:00:05.850 --> 00:00:09.100
life on silicon wafers like this one.

00:00:09.560 --> 00:00:13.530
But for future electronics
and quantum technologies,

00:00:13.900 --> 00:00:18.870
we're exploring materials
that are much, much thinner,

00:00:19.700 --> 00:00:23.870
sometimes just one molecular layer thick.

00:00:24.090 --> 00:00:27.970
These materials are called van der Waals
materials or two-dimensional

00:00:27.970 --> 00:00:32.680
materials. And their properties
can vary across regions barely

00:00:32.680 --> 00:00:35.830
the width of a single human hair.

00:00:36.560 --> 00:00:40.380
We're introducing to you in this tutorial
a technique called imaging

00:00:40.380 --> 00:00:45.020
spectroscopic ellipsometry,
specifically on the case of van der Waals

00:00:45.020 --> 00:00:45.790
materials.

00:00:46.650 --> 00:00:51.390
It is a non-contact optical technique
that uses polarised

00:00:51.390 --> 00:00:56.350
light that is reflected off from the
sample of interest and the

00:00:56.350 --> 00:00:59.620
polarisation changes
in the reflected light are measured.

00:01:00.850 --> 00:01:01.800
Two quantities,

00:01:02.470 --> 00:01:04.140
Psi and Delta,

00:01:05.040 --> 00:01:09.790
describe relative reflection amplitude
changes and wave phase differences.

00:01:13.970 --> 00:01:19.030
An imaging detector gives us
on top of that at each pixel

00:01:19.030 --> 00:01:21.300
these two parameters

00:01:22.370 --> 00:01:26.890
preventing an averaging of our area or
sample of interest with its

00:01:26.890 --> 00:01:30.030
environment. And then repeating these
measurements across different

00:01:30.030 --> 00:01:33.960
wavelengths builds a whole
hyperspectral data set.

00:01:34.170 --> 00:01:38.470
To however extract actual thicknesses or
optical constants from this

00:01:38.470 --> 00:01:41.460
measurement, we need
to use an optical model.

00:01:41.810 --> 00:01:46.450
We need to fit this optical model of the
entire stack to our

00:01:46.450 --> 00:01:47.640
ellipsometric data.

00:01:47.770 --> 00:01:52.690
And there, unfortunately, different
assumptions can produce similarly

00:01:52.690 --> 00:01:53.560
good fits.

00:01:54.510 --> 00:01:59.390
This is why we need to calibrate
the optical model and constrain it

00:01:59.390 --> 00:02:04.270
as much as we can with previously
known measurement parameters.

00:02:04.590 --> 00:02:09.550
For example, we can measure thicknesses
with atomic force microscopy and

00:02:09.550 --> 00:02:11.420
fix them in our optical model.

00:02:12.190 --> 00:02:17.050
And we can use Raman spectroscopy
to identify the actual chemical nature

00:02:17.050 --> 00:02:19.260
of the materials on our sample.

00:02:20.280 --> 00:02:23.100
Each of these techniques
measure different properties.

00:02:23.250 --> 00:02:27.930
Small spreads in fitted values
cannot really reveal how

00:02:27.930 --> 00:02:30.440
uncertain we were with our optical model.

00:02:30.470 --> 00:02:35.470
Our aim is to teach you that you can
obtain numbers easily with

00:02:35.470 --> 00:02:40.350
ellipsometry but you really
need to know when you can trust them.
